.ft TI
This developer edition independently adapts the Shark File System (SFS) lab
from CMU 15-213/15-513 (Summer 2024) for self-study.  Original materials and
lab design remain the property of Carnegie Mellon University and course staff.
This is not an official release; keep solutions private.
.ft R
.ps \n(PS
.vs \n(VSp
.ll 6.5i
.in 0
.sp 0.6v
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------- body
.SEC "Introduction"
.PP
A file system is a data structure that the operating system uses to store,
maintain, and refer to meaningful data on the disk, and conversely to derive
usable information from the raw data on the disk.  File systems allow
programs to interact with data as abstract files, leaving the cumbersome
work of putting that data onto the physical disk to lower-level calls.  File
systems are also simple but powerful tools for organization (with folders,
linking, etc.) and security (with permissions).
.PP
In this lab, we provide you with a very basic file system, which does not
implement folders, linking, or permissions.  In the Shark File System, as
distributed in this lab, you may only create and delete files, and read or
write to them.
.PP
The lab has two parts.  The first part deals with file system capabilities:
you will implement a few functions that introduce new abilities of the file
system, allowing users to see and change the position of file descriptors,
and to rename files with a single call.
.PP
The second part asks you to improve the file system's safety and
performance.  Currently, there is nothing to prevent two simultaneous calls
to the file system from overwriting or otherwise conflicting with each
other.  You will prevent conflicts like these from happening, while still
allowing concurrent and fast access to the file system for separate users.
.SEC "Logistics"
.PP
This edition of the lab is self-contained and self-graded: there is no
Autolab, no handin directory, and no course server.  The test driver
described in Section 6 plays the role of the autograder, and the score it
prints is local feedback, not an official CMU grade.
.PP
The scored student work remains confined to
.CW sfs-disk.c ;
do not modify
.CW sfs-api.h .
Use \f(CRmain\fP for the course-shaped edition.  This branch changes the
public API and disk format only for unscored extensions; treat them as
provided infrastructure.
.PP
The lab is intended for a 64-bit GNU/Linux environment (a native
installation, WSL2, or a Linux container) with
.CW gcc ,
.CW make ,
and POSIX threads.
.SEC "Handout Instructions"
.PP
Start by extracting the handout tarball in a directory where you plan to do
your work:
.CS
linux> tar xf sfslab-handout.tar
linux> cd sfslab
linux> make
.CE
.LP
The important files are:
.LB "\f(CRsfs-disk.c\fP"
The SFS implementation, and the only file you need to edit.  Three functions
in it are unimplemented, and the whole file is thread-unsafe until you make
it otherwise.
.LB "\f(CRsfs-api.h\fP"
The API specification.  The doc comment above each function tells you
exactly what that function must do.  Read it carefully before writing any
code.
.LB "\f(CRsfs-disk.h\fP"
The on-disk data structures and constants.  Worth reading: it defines the
high-level design of the file system.
.LB "\f(CRsfs-support.c\fP"
Low-level disk image and mmap support routines.  Provided; do not modify.
.LB "\f(CRtest-sfs.c\fP"
The test driver and autograder (Section 6).
.LB "\f(CRsfs-fsck.c\fP"
A file system consistency checker (Section 7.2).
.LB "\f(CRsfs-baseline-ref.c\fP"
The handout implementation wrapped in a single global mutex.  Used by
.CW "make baseline"
to calibrate the performance score for your machine.  Do not modify.
.EL
.PP
Typing
.CW make
produces three executables:
.CW test-sfs
(the driver),
.CW sfs-fsck
(the checker), and
.CW test-sfs-baseline
(the calibration reference).  Before you implement anything, run
.CW ./test-sfs
once: traces A00, A01, B00, and all of Category C should already pass, and
the traces that exercise
.CW sfs_getpos ,
.CW sfs_seek ,
and
.CW sfs_rename
should fail.  That is the expected starting state, and your job is to make
the rest of the scoreboard green.
.SEC "The SFS File System"
.SUB "The on-disk format"
.PP
An SFS disk image is an ordinary file, mapped into memory with
.CW mmap (2)
and divided into 512-byte blocks.  Block 0 is the
.I superblock ;
it identifies the image (a magic number), records the total number of
blocks, and embeds the root directory: an array of 15 fixed-size directory
entries, each holding a file name (at most 23 characters plus a terminating
NUL), the file's size in bytes, and the block number of the file's first
block.  A directory entry whose first-block field is zero is unused.  When
those entries fill, the developer branch chains additional directory blocks
through the superblock's
.CW next_rootdir
field.
.PP
Every other block begins with a 12-byte header containing a type tag and
the block numbers of the previous and next blocks in whatever chain the
block belongs to.  The remaining 500 bytes of each block hold file data.
Blocks form doubly linked lists: every file is a chain of blocks, and all
unallocated blocks sit on a free list rooted in the superblock.  Block
number 0 acts as the NULL pointer, so the superblock can never appear in
the middle of a chain.  This is the same flavor of design as the classic
FAT file system, except that the links live inside the blocks themselves.
.PP
A developer-branch empty file owns no data block; its first-block field is
the reserved \f(CRSFS_EMPTY_FILE_BLOCK\fP sentinel.  Nonempty files use one
block per 500 bytes.  The size field and the last block's zero next pointer
both mark the end of a file.
.SUB "The provided API"
.PP
The public entry points, declared and specified in
.CW sfs-api.h ,
are:
.LB "\f(CRsfs_format(diskName, diskSize)\fP"
Create and format a fresh disk image, then make it the active image.
.LB "\f(CRsfs_mount(diskName)\fP / \f(CRsfs_unmount()\fP"
Load an existing image / write out and deactivate the current one.
Unmounting fails with
.CW \-EBUSY
if any files are open.
.LB "\f(CRsfs_open(fileName)\fP"
Open a file, creating it if necessary; returns a small nonnegative
\(lqfile descriptor\(rq usable only with these routines.  Empty names return
.CW \-EINVAL ;
failed creates leave no directory entry or allocated block.
.LB "\f(CRsfs_close(fd)\fP"
Close a descriptor.  Never fails.
.LB "\f(CRsfs_read(fd, buf, len)\fP / \f(CRsfs_write(fd, buf, len)\fP"
Read or write up to
.CW len
bytes at the descriptor's current position, advancing the position.  Writes
extend the file (and allocate blocks) as needed.
.LB "\f(CRsfs_fstat(fd)\fP / \f(CRsfs_ftruncate(fd, length)\fP"
Developer-branch, unscored extensions: query a file's size, or resize it with
zero-fill on growth and block reclamation on shrink.
.LB "\f(CRsfs_remove(name)\fP"
Delete a file.  On the developer branch an open file is unlinked immediately,
remains usable through existing descriptors, and is reclaimed after the last
close.
.LB "\f(CRsfs_list(cookie, filename_out, filename_space)\fP"
Iterate over the directory, returning one file name per call.
.EL
.PP
All functions return 0 or a nonnegative result on success and a negative
errno value on failure.  The doc comments in
.CW sfs-api.h
are the authoritative specification, including the exact error codes the
driver checks for.
.SUB "The functions you must implement"
.PP
Three functions in
.CW sfs-disk.c
currently return
.CW \-ENOSYS .
Implementing them is your first order of business.
.LB "\f(CRssize_t sfs_getpos(int fd)\fP"
Return the current position of descriptor
.CW fd ,
in bytes from the start of the file.  If
.CW fd
is not a valid descriptor, return
.CW \-EBADF ;
this is the only possible error.  This function is a warm-up to get you
reading the descriptor bookkeeping in
.CW sfs-disk.c .
.LB "\f(CRssize_t sfs_seek(int fd, ssize_t delta)\fP"
Move \f(CRfd\fP's position by \f(CRdelta\fP bytes and return the result.
Clamp it to [0, file size], unlike \f(CRlseek(2)\fP.  The descriptor caches
both byte position and current block, so crossing block boundaries requires
walking the chain and keeping both fields consistent.  Given a prior
\f(CRsfs_getpos\fP result \f(CRloc\fP,
.CW "sfs_seek(fd, loc \- sfs_getpos(fd))"
must return the descriptor to the earlier position.
.LB "\f(CRint sfs_rename(const char *old_name, const char *new_name)\fP"
Rename a file.  If
.CW new_name
already exists, atomically delete and replace it: there must be no window
in which a concurrent thread can observe
.CW new_name
not existing.  For the single-threaded version this is straightforward;
once you add locking, keeping this promise is part of the design work.
Renaming an existing file to itself is a no-op; empty names return
.CW \-EINVAL .
.EL
.SEC "Making the File System Concurrent"
.PP
Once the three functions above work, the second half of the lab is to make
the file system safe and fast under concurrent use.  We suggest approaching
this in three gradations.
.PP
First, make the system thread-safe in a very coarse way, ensuring
correctness only.  This can be done by locking the entire file system
around every API call, exactly as the calibration reference
.CW sfs-baseline-ref.c
does.  Expect a performance ratio near 1.0\(mu and a performance score of
3/10 in the optional benchmark (Section 6.3).  A correct global-lock
implementation completes the basic exercise; optimization is a follow-up.
.PP
Second, think about readers and writers.  When reading, no shared data
changes except the reading descriptor's own position, so multiple readers
of the same file can proceed together, whereas writing wants exclusivity.
Be careful about where a reader/writer lock actually helps: because
.CW sfs_read
advances the descriptor's position, a single global rwlock buys almost
nothing \(em nearly every operation still needs the write side.
Reader/writer locks pay off for structures that are genuinely read-mostly,
such as the directory during name lookups, usually in combination with the
per-file locks below.
.PP
Third, exploit separate files.  Operations on different files touch
disjoint data (apart from the shared allocator and tables), so reading or
writing one file should not block work on another.  A lock per directory
slot (or per open file), plus short critical sections on the genuinely
shared structures \(em the free list, the directory, and the open-file
tables \(em is the level of granularity the performance score is calibrated
around.
.PP
Whatever design you choose, fix a global lock
.I order
and stick to it everywhere; two threads that take the same locks in
opposite orders will deadlock.  The driver's 30-second per-trace timeout
will catch a deadlock, but a design that cannot deadlock is far more
pleasant to debug.
.PP
.B "Developer edition:"
\f(CRD00\fP and \f(CRX00\fP through \f(CRX02\fP exercise file sizing,
zero-block empty files, directory growth, and Unix unlink.  They do not affect
the 22-point score.
.bp
.SEC "Evaluation"
.LP
The basic exercise requires 12 correctness points.  The optional benchmark
adds 10 performance points and 4 manual style points; D and X remain optional:
.TS
center box tab(;);
lb cb lb
l c l.
Category;Points;What it tests
_
A: feature traces (A00\-A04);5;T{
format/mount, open/close/read/write, getpos, seek, rename
T}
B: sequential traces (B00\-B03);4;T{
remove/list and slot reuse, seek/overwrite/append integrity,
ENOSPC/EMFILE rollback, name/size edges, rename/list
T}
C: concurrent traces (C00\-C02);3;T{
concurrent writes to separate files, concurrent reads of a shared file,
mixed read/write, an open/close storm, and directory churn
(create/rename/remove) under a live listing
T}
Performance;10;concurrent throughput against a calibrated baseline
Style;4;manual self-review (not auto-graded)
.TE
.SUB "Correctness (12 points)"
.PP
Each trace is worth one point; the driver prints a PASS/FAIL line per trace
followed by the details of any failed checks.  After every trace unmounts
its disk image, the driver also runs
.CW sfs-fsck
on the image: a trace fails if the visible API results look right but the
on-disk structure is corrupt (orphaned blocks, inconsistent links, sizes
that disagree with the allocation).  The first checker diagnostic is
included in the failure output.
.PP
Category C makes genuinely concurrent calls, including on systems without
ThreadSanitizer.  The test driver does not supply locks for your code.
Failures on the unlocked starter may vary between runs.  Start with one
mutex around the API, then refine only after correctness passes.
The listing trace always finishes each directory walk before starting another,
so holding a directory lock across an entire walk is supported.
.PP
Every trace \(em A and B included \(em runs in a forked child with a
30-second wall-clock budget.  A deadlock, crash, or assertion failure in
your code therefore fails that one trace (reported as TIMEOUT or CRASH)
instead of taking down the driver.
.SUB "Race detection"
.PP
After all twelve correctness traces pass, the driver attempts a
ThreadSanitizer build and reruns Category C under three schedule-fuzz seeds.
Data races and functional failures fail the concurrency check.  The sanitizer
has a 120-second budget per sweep, covering the three 30-second traces plus
startup overhead.  Failure diagnostics remain in tsan_output.log.
.PP
If the runtime cannot start, it is reported as unavailable without deducting
points.  Normal concurrent tests still run, but passing tests cannot prove
that no race exists.  Use a TSan-capable environment for additional checking.
.SUB "Performance (10 points)"
.PP
The optional performance benchmark runs with make grade or
./test-sfs --benchmark, and only after correctness is 12/12.  It starts
8 threads, each working on its own file.  A thread performs 16000
.I sessions ;
one session is one
.CW sfs_open ,
then 10 rounds of write/getpos/seek/read, then one
.CW sfs_close
\(em 5,376,000 API calls per run in total.  Timing starts immediately before
a start gate releases the already-created threads, so thread startup is
excluded; the benchmark is sampled
5 times and the median ops/sec is scored.
.PP
The workload validates its own results as it runs: every return value is
checked against the position the descriptor must be at, and every round seeks
back and compares the exact bytes it just wrote.  If any check
fails, the performance score is 0 \(em an implementation that is fast but
wrong on the benchmark path earns nothing.
.PP
Raw throughput is machine-dependent, so the score is a ratio against the
provided baseline (the handout implementation under one global mutex),
measured on
.I your
machine by
.CW "make baseline" :
.TS
center box tab(;);
cb cb lb
c c l.
Ratio;Score;Interpretation
_
\(>= 2.50;10/10;scaling that requires fine-grained locking
\(>= 1.80;9/10;strong parallelism
\(>= 1.40;7/10;meaningful parallelism
\(>= 1.20;5/10;measurably better than one global lock
\(>= 0.85;3/10;coarse-lock territory (this \fIis\fP the baseline)
< 0.85;0/10;slower than the naive baseline
.TE
.LP
These thresholds are local comparison targets, not verified official grades.
Core count, scheduling, and the machine affect achievable speedup.  Use the
ratio to compare your own designs; no speedup threshold is required to
complete the basic exercise.
.PP
If
.CW .perf_baseline
is missing, uses another workload version, or records a different
.CW SFS_DISK_DIR
than the scored run, the driver falls back to absolute throughput
.I "capped at 5/10"
and tells you to re-run
.CW "make baseline" .
Full performance credit always requires a calibrated ratio.
.SUB "Style (4 points)"
.PP
The 4 style points are a self-review: use the included
.CW .clang-format ,
remove dead code and debug output, explain
.I why
instead of what, clean up error paths, and keep locking locally auditable.
.SEC "Testing Your Work"
.SUB "The test driver"
.PP
Run \f(CR./test-sfs\fP for correctness, \f(CRmake developer-test\fP for
Category D, and \f(CRmake x-traces\fP for Category X.  D and X never change
the automatic score.  Use \f(CR-q\fP to hide FAIL detail and \f(CR-v\fP to
remove its default cap.  Exit status is 0 when all twelve correctness traces
pass, otherwise 1; performance never affects it.
.PP
In a non-quiet run, when a trace fails, the driver saves a copy of its
surviving disk
image(s) under
.CW fail_<trace>_<image>.img
and prints the path, so you can inspect the exact corrupted state instead
of re-running under a debugger.
The copies live in a private sfs-test.* directory, printed by the driver.
Remove that directory yourself after inspection, or set
.CW SFS_KEEP_FAILED_DISKS=0
to disable them.
.SUB "The consistency checker"
.PP
.CW sfs-fsck
verifies the structural integrity of a disk image: mislabeled blocks,
invalid directory entries, file sizes that disagree with the number of
allocated blocks, inconsistent or circular block chains, blocks on more
than one chain, and orphaned blocks on no chain at all.
On the developer branch its ownership map also scales past 250 files and it
accepts both the original one-block and developer zero-block empty-file
encodings.
.PP
The driver names its working images
.CW test.img
(Categories A and B),
.CW test_conc_*.img
(Category C), and
.CW test_perf.img
(the benchmark), inside a private sfs-test.* directory.  It removes
working images by the end of a run, preserving failure snapshots.  The image
you point the checker at is therefore usually a
.CW fail_*.img
snapshot from Section 7.1 \(em or any image your own experiments create
with
.CW sfs_format :
.CS
linux> ./sfs-fsck sfs-test.XXXXXX/fail_B01_test.img
linux> ./sfs-fsck -v sfs-test.XXXXXX/fail_B01_test.img
linux> ./sfs-fsck --dump sfs-test.XXXXXX/fail_B01_test.img
.CE
.LP
.CW \-\-dump
prints what the image actually contains \(em superblock summary, free list,
every directory entry with its block chain, and a block-type tally \(em
before judging it.  The dump is deliberately defensive: chains are walked
with a step cap and out-of-range block numbers are annotated rather than
crashing, so it stays useful on precisely the corrupted images you will
want to point it at.
.SUB "Race detection by hand"
.PP
The driver runs TSan for you, but reruns with full stack traces are often
more useful when hunting a specific race:
.CS
linux> gcc -std=c11 -g -fsanitize=thread -pthread -D_GNU_SOURCE=1 \(rs
           -I. -o test-sfs-tsan test-sfs.c sfs-disk.c sfs-support.c
linux> ./test-sfs-tsan --tsan-only --sched-fuzz=2
.CE
.LP
Replace
.CW 2
with whatever seed the driver reported.  The same seed requests similar perturbations; OS scheduling can still
change the execution, so rerunning need not reproduce a failure.  The
normal correctness pass never fuzzes; only the TSan sweep and explicit
.CW \-\-sched-fuzz
invocations do.
.SUB "Calibrating and reading the performance score"
.PP
Calibrate once per machine with
.CW "make baseline" ,
then run
.CW "./test-sfs --benchmark" ;
.CW "make grade"
does both.  The calibration prints a
.CW spread
line; if min and max differ by more than about 10%, the machine was not
quiet \(em close the browser and re-run.  Use
.CW "make baseline BASELINE_RUNS=11"
for a tighter (odd) sample count on noisy machines.
.SUB "Notes for Docker and WSL users"
.PP
Bind-mounted disk I/O can distort the benchmark.  Run
\f(CRexport SFS_DISK_DIR=/tmp\fP before both \f(CRmake baseline\fP and
\f(CR./test-sfs --benchmark\fP.  The driver records the setting in
\f(CR.perf_baseline\fP; a mismatch disables ratio scoring and caps performance
at 5/10.
.SEC "Hints"
.BU
Implement
.CW sfs_getpos
first \(em it is nearly free once you find the right field \(em then
.CW sfs_seek ,
then
.CW sfs_rename .
Re-run
.CW ./test-sfs
after each one.
.BU
Read the block-walking loops in
.CW sfs_read
and
.CW sfs_write
before writing
.CW sfs_seek ;
your seek must maintain the same descriptor invariants those loops rely
on (position and cached current block agreeing with each other).
.BU
Start the concurrency work coarse, verify correctness, then refine.  A
correct slow file system outscores a fast corrupt one by a mile: the
performance benchmark does not even run until correctness is 12/12.
.BU
Run
.CW sfs-fsck
after concurrent tests.  Races often corrupt the block chains in ways
that reads happen not to notice; the checker notices.
.BU
Be careful with the operations that touch global state: the free list,
the directory, and the open-file tables.  Each can have its own small
lock, but fix a lock order.
.EL
.APP "Appendix: Frequently Asked Questions"
.LB "\(lqNo space left on device\(rq"
Each test sizes its disk image appropriately for correct execution.  If
your implementation leaks blocks or double-allocates under a race, an
operation can fail with
.CW \-ENOSPC
even though a correct implementation would have room.  Run
.CW sfs-fsck
on the failing image; leaked blocks show up as \(lqnot on any block
list.\(rq
.LB "How do I debug disk corruption?"
.CW ./sfs-fsck\ -v
narrates the whole check, and
.CW \-\-dump
shows the structure it found.  The
.CW fail_*.img
snapshots the driver keeps for failing traces are exactly the images to
inspect.
.LB "What if \f(CRsfs_seek\fP goes past the end of the file?"
Clamp to the file size; if it would go negative, clamp to 0.  This is
specified in
.CW sfs-api.h
and is deliberately different from
.CW lseek (2).
.EL
